\f1\b \cf0 Question:
\f0\b0  Explain Restoring and Non-Restoring division algorithms. Include step-by-step procedures and examples.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 Answer:
\f0\b0 \

\f1\b Restoring Division
\f0\b0  (Slides 100-101):\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls21\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 From Slide 100: "Flowchart for Restoring Division"\
\ls21\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 From Slide 101: "Restoring Division \'96 Example"\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls21\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Procedure
\f0\b0  (from standard algorithm):\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 1. Initialize: A = 0, Q = Dividend, M = Divisor, count = n (number of bits)\
2. For each bit position:\
a. Shift AQ left 1 bit\
b. A = A - M\
c. If A \uc0\u8805  0: Set Q
\fs20 \sub 0
\fs24 \nosupersub  = 1 (quotient bit = 1)\
Else: Set Q
\fs20 \sub 0
\fs24 \nosupersub  = 0, and restore A = A + M (undo the subtraction)\
d. Decrement count\
3. Quotient is in Q, Remainder is in A\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 Non-Restoring Division
\f0\b0  (Slides 102-105):\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls22\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 From Slide 102: "Non-Restoring Division Initially Dividend is loaded into register Q, and n-bit Divisor is loaded into register M Let M\'92 is 2\'92s complement of M Set Register A to 0 Set count to n SHL AQ denotes shift left AQ by one position leaving Q 0 blank. Similarly, a square symbol in Q 0 position denote, it is to be calculated later"\
\ls22\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 From Slide 103: "Flowchart for Non-Restoring Division"\
\ls22\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 From Slide 105: "Non-Restoring Division - Example"\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls22\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Procedure
\f0\b0 :\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 1. Initialize: A = 0, Q = Dividend, M = Divisor, M' = -M (2's complement), count = n\
2. For each bit position:\
a. Shift AQ left 1 bit\
b. If previous step was successful (A \uc0\u8805  0): A = A - M\
Else: A = A + M' (add instead of subtract)\
c. If A \uc0\u8805  0: Set Q
\fs20 \sub 0
\fs24 \nosupersub  = 1\
Else: Set Q
\fs20 \sub 0
\fs24 \nosupersub  = 0\
d. Decrement count\
3. After all bits processed:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls23\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 If A \uc0\u8805  0: Quotient = Q, Remainder = A\
\ls23\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 If A < 0: Quotient = Q, Remainder = A + M (final correction)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 4. Quotient is in Q, Final remainder is in A (after correction if needed)\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 Key Difference
\f0\b0 : Restoring division may need to restore A after each unsuccessful subtraction, while Non-restoring division avoids this immediate restoration by using a different approach that only requires at most one correction at the end.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
